{"ts": 1789770943.6042292, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I've searched thoroughly and the picture is clear. Let me submit my best specification with an honest forecast.\n\nMy reasoning about what the OOS Sharpe will be:\n\n**The in-sample 0.861 is an upward-biased estimate.** I selected it as the max over roughly 120 evaluated specifications, drawn from a space of ~10,000 possible signed triples. Two distinct sources of shrinkage apply:\n\n1. **Selection bias.** With SE \u2248 0.22 per Sharpe estimate, taking the max over many correlated candidates inflates the estimate. The plateau structure is diagnostic: the top eight triples span 0.78\u20130.86, a range well inside one SE. That means the *ranking* among them is essentially noise, and 0.861 is the lucky draw from that cluster rather than a genuinely superior spec. The plateau's center (~0.80) is a better estimate of the winner's true quality than its peak.\n\n2. **Sign-fitting.** I chose signs using the same data. The sign flip check confirms signs are fully determined by the data (exact \u22120.861), so any sign that was fitted to noise will not replicate.\n\nOffsetting this, the core signal looks partly real rather than pure mining: features 20, 16, and 34 were each independently positive as singles, they combine with gains consistent with genuine diversification, and 20+16 reached 0.770 with only two pre-specified choices. So I expect meaningful positive OOS performance \u2014 just materially below 0.861.\n\nMy estimate: shrink to roughly **0.6**, with wide uncertainty (sd 0.25) reflecting both estimation error and the possibility that more of the edge than I think is artifact."}
