{"ts": 1789771004.71054, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** Long features 20, 16, 34 (equal weight). In-sample Sharpe **0.861**. Predicted OOS: **0.60 \u00b1 0.25**.\n\n### Search process\n1. Evaluated all 40 features individually. Most were statistically indistinguishable from zero; strongest were f20 (+0.59), f16 (+0.52), f34 (+0.39), f21 (\u22120.38), f1 (\u22120.38), f33 (+0.37).\n2. Tested 16 pairs among the strongest signed singles \u2014 best was (20,16) at 0.770.\n3. Swept **all 38** remaining features as a third leg on (20,16), plus alternative triples from other top pairs.\n\n### Key judgment: I did not clear the bar, and I'm reporting that honestly\n\nThe search-adjusted bar is **1.024**; my best is **0.861**. I chose not to keep mining for something that would nominally cross it, because at this point additional search would almost certainly produce an *apparent* winner that is pure overfitting. The bar is set where it is precisely to account for the ~10,000 signed triples available \u2014 clearing it by drawing more samples from that space would be a false positive, not a discovery.\n\nTwo pieces of evidence drove my forecast down from 0.861:\n\n- **The top of the distribution is a flat plateau.** The eight best triples span 0.78\u20130.86, a range smaller than one standard error (SE \u2248 0.22 with n=5000 daily obs). The ordering within that group is noise, so 0.861 is the lucky maximum of the cluster, not a genuinely better spec. I anchored my estimate nearer the plateau's center.\n- **Signs were fitted in-sample.** The exact sign-flip result (\u22120.861) confirms the data fully determines the signs, so any sign fitted to noise won't replicate.\n\n### Why I still expect positive OOS performance\nThe core signal appears partly genuine rather than entirely mined: features 20, 16, and 34 were each independently positive as singles *before* any combination search, and their gains when combined track what ordinary diversification of weakly-correlated signals would predict. The pair (20,16) hit 0.770 using only two pre-specified choices, which is hard to attribute to selection.\n\nMy 0.60 estimate reflects roughly a 30% haircut for selection and sign-fitting. The wide sd of 0.25 covers both ordinary estimation error and the real possibility that more of this edge is artifact than I judge \u2014 the interval comfortably admits outcomes near 0.1 as well as near 1.0."}
